    {
      "id": "jvxm4PkmIatD0im1ARUwB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 995.9588449001772,
      "y": 5422.843976695502,
      "width": 869.5596313476562,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1209521477,
      "version": 1707,
      "versionNonce": 490860464,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1770016590213,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Now we implement a controlled leak. The background worker in the waiting room service or another service\nconsumes tokens from Kafka at a fixed rate, matching downstream capacity, say, 500 tokens / sec and marks\nthe corresponding token status as active in a dedicated redis cluster and updates the global \"serving upto\" id,\nsuch that when a client checks again, their local <= serving and it will redirect them to the main site.\n\nThe API gateway can again revalidate against the Redis, to ensure that the token is now valid and active\nbefore allowing them access to the main site.\n\nSo combination of waiting room + Kafka queueing + Redis tokens, we handle the massive spiky traffic by\ncontrolling the flow of users into our system - 4",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now we implement a controlled leak. The background worker in the waiting room service or another service consumes tokens from Kafka at a fixed rate, matching downstream capacity, say, 500 tokens / sec and marks the corresponding token status as active in a dedicated redis cluster and updates the global \"serving upto\" id, such that when a client checks again, their local <= serving and it will redirect them to the main site.\n\nThe API gateway can again revalidate against the Redis, to ensure that the token is now valid and active before allowing them access to the main site.\n\nSo combination of waiting room + Kafka queueing + Redis tokens, we handle the massive spiky traffic by controlling the flow of users into our system - 4",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
